The wal pusher runs during recovery.

A crash leaves local pg_wal ahead of the stream, because the pusher
died with everything else before it had drained. Recovery then reads
pages from the page service at the lsn it is replaying, the service
can only serve what the store holds, the read parks for the wait cap
and fails, and the startup process dies with it. Three restarts later
the postmaster gives up and the node is gone for good.

The pusher already knows how to close that gap: on a store whose
stream ends behind local flush it re-pushes the difference. It just
ran too late, registered with BgWorkerStart_RecoveryFinished, waiting
on the recovery that was waiting on it. It now starts with the
postmaster, like the page service, and trails replay while recovery
runs.

Out of recovery its bound is the flush pointer as before. In recovery
there is no flush pointer, GetFlushRecPtr asserts on that, and the
bytes known good are the ones replay has read, so the bound is
lastReplayedEndRecPtr. Recovery reads its pages at that same lsn, so a
parked read costs one push and one ingest poll instead of the wait
cap. A store that ends ahead of the replay pointer is the ordinary
case during recovery rather than the fatal one it is afterwards, and
folding waits until recovery is done.

Taking the writer lease before recovery instead of after comes with
this, and it is the right way round. A cluster that has lost the lease
holds a branch nobody will read, and it should learn that before it
spends a recovery, not after.

diff --git a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
